<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bristol</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bristol is a city and port in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the River Avon, near its mouth on the Severn estuary. A major trading town since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cabot">John Cabot</a>'s expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1497. In the eighteenth century it was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s leading ports in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and the trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and tobacco, and it later became an industrial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bristol.jpg" alt="Bristol" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Lathom-Sharp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clifton_Suspension_Bridge_and_the_Observatory_in_Bristol,_England.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bristol lies on the River Avon, which cuts through a limestone area running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendip-hills">Mendip Hills</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotswolds">Cotswolds</a> and gives the city its hilly landscape. To the west the Avon passes through the Avon Gorge, formed largely by glacial meltwater after the last ice age. The name comes from the Old English Brycgstow, meaning the assembly place by the bridge or the site of the bridge, presumably a crossing of the Avon. The final L is a later addition that first appears in the 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Roman port, Portus Abonae, existed at what is now Sea Mills. Bristol was founded by 1000, and by about 1020 it was a trading centre with a mint producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> pennies bearing its name. By 1067 it was a well-fortified burh, and that year the townsmen beat back a raiding party from Ireland led by three sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>. Its Norman castle was one of the strongest in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The town received a royal charter in 1155 and became a county corporate in 1373, having until then been divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>.</p><p>In the 15th century Bristol was the second most important port in the country, trading with Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. John Cabot made landfall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1497, and a voyage of 1499 led by the Bristol merchant William Weston was the first expedition commanded by an Englishman to North America. Between 1700 and 1807 more than 2,000 slave ships carried an estimated 500,000 people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. In the 19th century Isambard Kingdom Brunel designed the Great Western Railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the SS Great Britain and the Clifton Suspension Bridge. Competition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1793 and the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in 1807 contributed to Bristol's failure to keep pace with northern manufacturing centres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bristol is a city and ceremonial county in South West England and the most populous city in its region. The Port of Bristol has moved from the harbour in the city centre downstream, and the old docks have been redeveloped as cultural and heritage centres. The city has two universities, the University of Bristol and the University of the West of England, and 51 Grade I listed buildings, among them the Church of St James, which dates to the 12th century. A dialect known as Bristolian is spoken by longtime residents.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bristol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ships from London, <b>Bristol</b> and Liverpool dominated the Atlantic slave trade, carrying more than two million Africans to the Americas during the century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The movement was strongest where working people depended on single industries and suffered wild swings in economic activity, and weaker in places with more varied economies, such as <b>Bristol</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/chartism">Chartism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In England Whitefield, refused pulpits in Bath and <b>Bristol</b> in February 1739, began field preaching at Kingswood and within a week drew crowds of 10,000; by May he was preaching to London crowds of 50,000.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Libertarian was already in print in 1796, when the London Packet reported 450 “French Libertarians” marching out of the prison at <b>Bristol</b>, and there it meant a supporter of republicanism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/libertarianism">Libertarianism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Percy Grainger recorded the London singer Charles Rosher in 1906, and the former shantyman Stanley Slade of <b>Bristol</b> was recorded in 1943.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-shenandoah">Oh Shenandoah</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States John Lomax recorded a related version, &quot;The Cambric Shirt&quot;, from Georgia Ann Griffin in Florida in 1939, and other field recordings followed in Arkansas (1958), New York (1966) and <b>Bristol</b> (1976).</blockquote><a href="/songs/scarborough-fair">Scarborough Fair</a></li></ul></section>
